# HostelFinder — Full Code Walkthrough

Companion to [EXPLANATION.md](EXPLANATION.md) (which covers the *why*). This
document goes file-by-file through every class in the backend with the actual
code and a line-level explanation, in the order a request would actually flow
through the system. Read this to be able to answer "walk me through this file"
for anything in the repo.

**Reading order**: `auth` (login/signup) → `config` (how every other request
gets authenticated) → `college` (simplest CRUD, the template pattern) →
`hostel` (search/filter/distance/images) → `booking` (the concurrency-critical
part) → `common` (cross-cutting glue).

---

## 1. `auth` package

### 1.1 `Role.java` — the two-value source of truth

```java
public enum Role {
    ADMIN,
    STUDENT
}
```
Everything downstream — JWT claims, Spring Security authorities, route
guards — derives from this one enum. There's no third role and no
inheritance between them; they're deliberately treated as unrelated actor
types that happen to share a login mechanism.

### 1.2 `AdminUser.java` / `AppUser.java` — the two user tables

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "admin_user")
@Getter @Setter @NoArgsConstructor
public class AdminUser {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String email;

    @Column(name = "password_hash", nullable = false)
    private String passwordHash;

    @Column(name = "created_at", nullable = false, updatable = false)
    private Instant createdAt = Instant.now();
}
```
Plain JPA entities. `@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)`
delegates ID generation to Postgres's `bigserial` (auto-increment) rather
than a separate sequence-fetch round trip — the simplest strategy, and fine
since we don't need pre-insert IDs for anything (e.g. no batch-insert
optimization needs here).

`passwordHash` is never the raw password — it's always the output of
`PasswordEncoder.encode(...)` (BCrypt), set once at signup/seed time and
compared (never decrypted — BCrypt is one-way) at login time by
`AuthenticationManager`.

`AppUser` is identical in shape but adds `name` and `phone` — fields that
only make sense for a student, which is the concrete reason these are two
separate tables rather than one `users` table with a `role` discriminator
column (see EXPLANATION.md §1.8 for the full reasoning).

### 1.3 `AdminUserRepository.java` / `AppUserRepository.java`

```java
public interface AdminUserRepository extends JpaRepository<AdminUser, Long> {
    Optional<AdminUser> findByEmail(String email);
}

public interface AppUserRepository extends JpaRepository<AppUser, Long> {
    Optional<AppUser> findByEmail(String email);
    boolean existsByEmail(String email);
}
```
Spring Data JPA generates the implementation of these at runtime purely from
the method name — `findByEmail` becomes `SELECT * FROM app_user WHERE
email = ?`, `existsByEmail` becomes a cheap `SELECT EXISTS(...)` rather than
fetching a whole row just to check presence (used in signup's duplicate-email
check).

### 1.4 `UserPrincipal.java` — the object that represents "who is making this request"

```java
@Getter
public class UserPrincipal implements UserDetails {
    private final Long id;
    private final String email;
    private final String passwordHash;
    private final Role role;

    public UserPrincipal(Long id, String email, String passwordHash, Role role) {
        this.id = id;
        this.email = email;
        this.passwordHash = passwordHash;
        this.role = role;
    }

    @Override
    public Collection<? extends GrantedAuthority> getAuthorities() {
        return List.of(new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + role.name()));
    }

    @Override
    public String getPassword() { return passwordHash; }

    @Override
    public String getUsername() { return email; }
}
```
This implements Spring Security's `UserDetails` interface — the contract
Spring Security uses internally to represent an authenticated principal,
regardless of whether it came from a database, an LDAP server, or (as here)
decoded straight out of a JWT.

The one line that matters most: `"ROLE_" + role.name()`. Spring Security's
`hasRole('ADMIN')` check (used in every `@PreAuthorize`) is sugar for
`hasAuthority('ROLE_ADMIN')` — it silently prepends `ROLE_` for you. This is
a very common source of "why isn't my role check working" bugs when people
forget the prefix is implicit — worth mentioning if asked about
gotchas.

Notice `UserPrincipal` is a **plain POJO, not a JPA entity** — it's a
security-layer view over either an `AdminUser` or an `AppUser`, built fresh
on every login and on every JWT-authenticated request (see 2.1). It doesn't
have a table of its own.

### 1.5 `AuthUserDetailsService.java` — login-time user lookup

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class AuthUserDetailsService implements UserDetailsService {
    private final AdminUserRepository adminUserRepository;
    private final AppUserRepository appUserRepository;

    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String email) throws UsernameNotFoundException {
        return adminUserRepository.findByEmail(email)
                .map(a -> (UserDetails) new UserPrincipal(a.getId(), a.getEmail(), a.getPasswordHash(), Role.ADMIN))
                .or(() -> appUserRepository.findByEmail(email)
                        .map(u -> new UserPrincipal(u.getId(), u.getEmail(), u.getPasswordHash(), Role.STUDENT)))
                .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException("No user with email: " + email));
    }
}
```
`UserDetailsService` is the interface Spring Security's
`DaoAuthenticationProvider` calls during `authenticationManager.authenticate(...)`.
The implementation here checks the admin table first, and if nothing
matches, falls through to the student table (`Optional.or(...)` — lazily
evaluated, so a matching admin never even triggers the student query).

**Important scope note**: this class is used **only during the login POST
requests** (`/api/auth/*/login`). It is *not* consulted on every
subsequent authenticated request — that's a different, cheaper path,
covered next.

### 1.6 `JwtService.java` — issuing and verifying tokens

```java
@Service
public class JwtService {
    private final SecretKey key;
    private final long expirationMs;

    public JwtService(@Value("${app.jwt.secret}") String secret,
                       @Value("${app.jwt.expiration-ms}") long expirationMs) {
        this.key = Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(secret.getBytes());
        this.expirationMs = expirationMs;
    }

    public String generateToken(UserPrincipal principal) {
        Date now = new Date();
        Date expiry = new Date(now.getTime() + expirationMs);
        return Jwts.builder()
                .subject(principal.getUsername())
                .claim("uid", principal.getId())
                .claim("role", principal.getRole().name())
                .issuedAt(now)
                .expiration(expiry)
                .signWith(key)
                .compact();
    }

    public Claims parseClaims(String token) {
        return Jwts.parser()
                .verifyWith(key)
                .build()
                .parseSignedClaims(token)
                .getPayload();
    }
}
```
`Keys.hmacShaKeyFor(secret.getBytes())` builds a symmetric HMAC signing key
from the config string `app.jwt.secret` (defaults to a dev placeholder,
overridable via `JWT_SECRET` env var — this is the *only* secret that
matters for token integrity; anyone with it can forge admin tokens, so in
production it must be a long random value from a secrets manager, not the
committed default).

`generateToken` puts three things in the payload: `sub` (subject = email, a
JWT-standard claim), and two custom claims — `uid` and `role` — which is
everything `JwtAuthFilter` needs to reconstruct a `UserPrincipal` without
touching the database (see 2.1). `signWith(key)` computes the HMAC signature
over the header+payload — this is what makes the token tamper-evident: if
anyone changes the `role` claim from `STUDENT` to `ADMIN` without knowing the
secret, `parseClaims` on the receiving end will throw a signature-verification
exception.

`parseClaims` does the inverse: verifies the signature (throws
`io.jsonwebtoken.security.SignatureException`, a subtype of `JwtException`,
if it doesn't match or the token is expired) and returns the decoded claims.

### 1.7 `AuthDtos.java` — request/response shapes

```java
public class AuthDtos {
    public record SignupRequest(
            @NotBlank String name,
            @NotBlank @Email String email,
            @NotBlank @Size(min = 8) String password,
            String phone
    ) {}

    public record LoginRequest(
            @NotBlank @Email String email,
            @NotBlank String password
    ) {}

    public record AuthResponse(
            String token,
            String role,
            Long userId,
            String email
    ) {}
}
```
Java `record`s used purely as immutable DTOs — no behavior, just data plus
Jakarta Bean Validation annotations. `@Valid` on the controller parameter
(see 1.8) triggers these checks automatically before the method body even
runs; a failure short-circuits into `GlobalExceptionHandler`'s
`MethodArgumentNotValidException` handler (see 6.1), returning a 400 with a
field-by-field error map — the controller and service code never see an
invalid request.

Nesting all the DTOs for a package inside one outer class (`AuthDtos`,
`CollegeDtos`, `HostelDtos`, `BookingDtos`) is a naming convention used
throughout this codebase to keep each package's `import static
...Dtos.*` clean and to avoid a proliferation of tiny top-level files.

### 1.8 `AuthService.java` — the actual login/signup logic

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class AuthService {
    private final AppUserRepository appUserRepository;
    private final AdminUserRepository adminUserRepository;
    private final PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder;
    private final AuthenticationManager authenticationManager;
    private final JwtService jwtService;

    public AuthResponse studentSignup(SignupRequest request) {
        if (appUserRepository.existsByEmail(request.email())) {
            throw new IllegalRequestException("Email already registered: " + request.email());
        }
        AppUser user = new AppUser();
        user.setName(request.name());
        user.setEmail(request.email());
        user.setPasswordHash(passwordEncoder.encode(request.password()));
        user.setPhone(request.phone());
        appUserRepository.save(user);

        UserPrincipal principal = new UserPrincipal(user.getId(), user.getEmail(), user.getPasswordHash(), Role.STUDENT);
        String token = jwtService.generateToken(principal);
        return new AuthResponse(token, Role.STUDENT.name(), user.getId(), user.getEmail());
    }
```
Signup: check-then-insert on email uniqueness (there's also a DB-level
`unique` constraint as the real backstop against a race between two
simultaneous signups with the same email — the app-level check is just for a
friendlier error message in the common case), hash the password, save, and
**immediately mint a token** — so a successful signup logs the student in
without a second round trip.

```java
    public AuthResponse studentLogin(LoginRequest request) {
        return login(request, Role.STUDENT);
    }

    public AuthResponse adminLogin(LoginRequest request) {
        return login(request, Role.ADMIN);
    }

    private AuthResponse login(LoginRequest request, Role expectedRole) {
        authenticationManager.authenticate(
                new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(request.email(), request.password()));

        UserPrincipal principal = switch (expectedRole) {
            case ADMIN -> adminUserRepository.findByEmail(request.email())
                    .map(a -> new UserPrincipal(a.getId(), a.getEmail(), a.getPasswordHash(), Role.ADMIN))
                    .orElseThrow(() -> new BadCredentialsException("Invalid credentials"));
            case STUDENT -> appUserRepository.findByEmail(request.email())
                    .map(u -> new UserPrincipal(u.getId(), u.getEmail(), u.getPasswordHash(), Role.STUDENT))
                    .orElseThrow(() -> new BadCredentialsException("Invalid credentials"));
        };

        String token = jwtService.generateToken(principal);
        return new AuthResponse(token, principal.getRole().name(), principal.getId(), principal.getUsername());
    }
```
This is doing two checks, not one, and the distinction matters:

1. `authenticationManager.authenticate(...)` — this is Spring Security's
   standard credential check. Internally it calls
   `AuthUserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(email)` (§1.5, which checks
   *both* tables), then compares the submitted password against the found
   user's `passwordHash` via `PasswordEncoder.matches(...)`. If the email
   doesn't exist anywhere, or the password is wrong, this throws
   `BadCredentialsException` on its own, before we even reach the `switch`.
2. **The `switch` on `expectedRole`** — this is a second, deliberate check:
   even if the password was correct, was the email found in the *table that
   matches the endpoint being called*? Without this, a student with a
   correct password could technically authenticate successfully against
   `/api/auth/admin/login` (since `AuthUserDetailsService` checks both
   tables) and — if step 1 alone decided the outcome — walk away with a
   token claiming `role: ADMIN`. The `switch` re-derives the principal from
   *only* the table matching `expectedRole`, so a student hitting the admin
   login endpoint gets a clean `BadCredentialsException` regardless of how
   correct their student password is.

### 1.9 `AuthController.java` — the HTTP layer

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/auth")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class AuthController {
    private final AuthService authService;

    @PostMapping("/student/signup")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public AuthResponse studentSignup(@Valid @RequestBody SignupRequest request) {
        return authService.studentSignup(request);
    }

    @PostMapping("/student/login")
    public AuthResponse studentLogin(@Valid @RequestBody LoginRequest request) {
        return authService.studentLogin(request);
    }

    @PostMapping("/admin/login")
    public AuthResponse adminLogin(@Valid @RequestBody LoginRequest request) {
        return authService.adminLogin(request);
    }
}
```
Deliberately thin — no logic, just routing + validation trigger
(`@Valid`) + delegation. This is the pattern every controller in the
codebase follows: **controllers never contain business logic**, they're a
translation layer between HTTP and the service layer. This makes the
service layer independently unit-testable without spinning up a servlet
container, and keeps the same business logic reusable if a second entry
point (e.g. a CLI, a batch job) ever needed it.

