## 2. `config` package — how every other request gets authenticated

### 2.1 `JwtAuthFilter.java` — runs once per request, before Spring Security's own filters

```java
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class JwtAuthFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
    private final JwtService jwtService;

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response, FilterChain filterChain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        String header = request.getHeader("Authorization");
        if (header != null && header.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
            String token = header.substring(7);
            try {
                Claims claims = jwtService.parseClaims(token);
                Long userId = claims.get("uid", Long.class);
                Role role = Role.valueOf(claims.get("role", String.class));
                UserPrincipal principal = new UserPrincipal(userId, claims.getSubject(), null, role);
                var authToken = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(principal, null, principal.getAuthorities());
                authToken.setDetails(new WebAuthenticationDetailsSource().buildDetails(request));
                SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(authToken);
            } catch (JwtException | IllegalArgumentException ignored) {
                SecurityContextHolder.clearContext();
            }
        }
        filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
}
```
Line by line:
- Extends `OncePerRequestFilter` — a Spring convenience base class that
  guarantees `doFilterInternal` runs exactly once per request even if the
  servlet container's dispatch mechanism would otherwise invoke the filter
  chain more than once (e.g. on an internal forward).
- Reads the raw `Authorization` header and strips the `Bearer ` prefix
  (7 characters) to get the token.
- `jwtService.parseClaims(token)` — this is where signature verification and
  expiry checking actually happen (§1.6). If the token is invalid, this
  throws, and the `catch` block clears the security context (so the request
  proceeds as *anonymous*, not rejected outright by this filter).
- If parsing succeeds, it rebuilds a `UserPrincipal` **entirely from the
  claims already inside the token** — `claims.get("uid", ...)`, `claims.get
  ("role", ...)`, `claims.getSubject()` for email. **No database query
  happens here.** This is the concrete meaning of "stateless auth": the
  filter doesn't ask "does this user still exist / what's their current
  role" — it trusts the signed token's claims, which is a deliberate
  performance/simplicity tradeoff (see EXPLANATION.md §2.2 for what this
  costs: a JWT keeps working even if the underlying user is deleted, until
  it naturally expires).
- `new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(principal, null, principal.getAuthorities())`
  — the second argument (credentials) is `null` because we're not
  re-checking a password here, just recording who's already been verified.
  This gets placed into `SecurityContextHolder`, which is thread-local
  storage Spring Security reads from for the rest of the request's
  processing (this is what `@AuthenticationPrincipal` and `@PreAuthorize`
  read from downstream).
- Critically, this filter **never rejects a request itself** — it either
  populates the security context or leaves it empty; the actual "you're not
  allowed here" decision is made later, by `SecurityConfig`'s
  `authorizeHttpRequests` rules and by `@PreAuthorize`.

### 2.2 `SecurityConfig.java` — wiring it all together

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class SecurityConfig {
    private final JwtAuthFilter jwtAuthFilter;

    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }

    @Bean
    public AuthenticationManager authenticationManager(AuthenticationConfiguration config) throws Exception {
        return config.getAuthenticationManager();
    }

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
                .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
                .sessionManagement(sm -> sm.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
                .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                        .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()
                        .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/api/colleges/**").permitAll()
                        .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/api/hostels/**").permitAll()
                        .anyRequest().authenticated()
                )
                .addFilterBefore(jwtAuthFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class);
        return http.build();
    }
}
```
- `@EnableMethodSecurity` turns on `@PreAuthorize` support — without this
  annotation on some `@Configuration` class, every `@PreAuthorize` in the
  codebase would be silently ignored.
- `passwordEncoder()` — a single `BCryptPasswordEncoder` bean, injected
  everywhere a password needs hashing or checking (`AuthService`,
  `AdminSeeder`). BCrypt is adaptive (configurable work factor) and
  includes a random salt per hash automatically, which is why two calls to
  `encode("changeit123")` produce different output strings — this is
  expected and correct.
- `csrf.disable()` — CSRF protection defends against a browser being tricked
  into submitting a form to your app using the *browser's own cookies* for
  auth. Since this API uses a JWT in an `Authorization` header (not a
  cookie) and is stateless, there's no ambient credential for CSRF to hijack
  — disabling it here is standard for token-based APIs, not a security
  shortcut.
- `sessionCreationPolicy(STATELESS)` — tells Spring Security to never create
  or use an `HttpSession`. Combined with no CSRF and JWT auth, this is what
  makes the API horizontally scalable without sticky sessions.
- `authorizeHttpRequests(...)` — the URL-level allow-list. Order matters:
  Spring evaluates these top-to-bottom and uses the *first* match, so the
  specific `GET` exceptions for `/api/colleges/**` and `/api/hostels/**`
  must come before the catch-all `anyRequest().authenticated()`. Everything
  not explicitly listed here — which includes `POST/PUT/DELETE` on those
  same paths, and all of `/api/bookings/**` — falls through to requiring
  *some* authenticated principal (role-specific checks happen one layer
  deeper, via `@PreAuthorize`).
- `addFilterBefore(jwtAuthFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)`
  — registers our custom filter to run before Spring's own default
  username/password filter in the chain, guaranteeing the security context
  is populated (or not) before any authorization decision gets made.

### 2.3 `AdminSeeder.java` — bootstrapping the first admin account

```java
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class AdminSeeder implements CommandLineRunner {
    private final AdminUserRepository adminUserRepository;
    private final PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder;

    @Value("${app.admin.seed-email:admin@hostelfinder.local}")
    private String seedEmail;

    @Value("${app.admin.seed-password:changeit123}")
    private String seedPassword;

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        if (adminUserRepository.findByEmail(seedEmail).isEmpty()) {
            AdminUser admin = new AdminUser();
            admin.setEmail(seedEmail);
            admin.setPasswordHash(passwordEncoder.encode(seedPassword));
            adminUserRepository.save(admin);
            log.info("Seeded default admin account: {}", seedEmail);
        }
    }
}
```
`CommandLineRunner` is a Spring Boot interface whose `run(...)` method fires
exactly once, automatically, right after the application context finishes
starting up (and Flyway migrations have already applied — Spring Boot runs
Flyway during context initialization, before any `CommandLineRunner`).
There's no public admin registration endpoint by design (§EXPLANATION.md
§1.6/2.1 note) — this is the *only* way an admin account comes into
existence, which is why "how do I change the admin password" requires either
deleting the row so this seeder recreates it, or updating the DB directly —
editing `application.yml` alone does nothing once the row already exists,
because of the `isEmpty()` guard.

