## 3. `college` package — the CRUD template

This package is the simplest slice in the codebase and demonstrates the
four-layer pattern (`Entity → Repository → Service → Controller`) used
throughout, without any of the extra complexity from search/concurrency/files.

### 3.1 `College.java`

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "college")
@Getter @Setter @NoArgsConstructor
public class College {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false) private String name;
    @Column(nullable = false) private String city;
    @Column(nullable = false) private double latitude;
    @Column(nullable = false) private double longitude;

    @Column(name = "created_at", nullable = false, updatable = false)
    private Instant createdAt = Instant.now();
}
```
Nothing unusual — `latitude`/`longitude` as plain `double` columns (not
PostGIS `geography` — see EXPLANATION.md §1.4 for why).

### 3.2 `CollegeRepository.java`

```java
public interface CollegeRepository extends JpaRepository<College, Long> {
}
```
No custom query methods needed — `findAll()`, `findById()`, `save()`,
`deleteById()`, `existsById()` all come for free from `JpaRepository`.

### 3.3 `CollegeDtos.java`

```java
public class CollegeDtos {
    public record CollegeRequest(
            @NotBlank String name,
            @NotBlank String city,
            @NotNull @Min(-90) @Max(90) Double latitude,
            @NotNull @Min(-180) @Max(180) Double longitude
    ) {}

    public record CollegeResponse(Long id, String name, String city, double latitude, double longitude) {
        public static CollegeResponse from(College college) {
            return new CollegeResponse(college.getId(), college.getName(), college.getCity(),
                    college.getLatitude(), college.getLongitude());
        }
    }
}
```
Note `latitude`/`longitude` are boxed `Double` in the request DTO (so
`@NotNull` can actually detect a missing field — a primitive `double` can't
be `null`, it silently defaults to `0.0`, which would pass validation
incorrectly) but primitive `double` in the entity and response (where a
value is always present, so there's no ambiguity to guard against). This is
a small but common gotcha worth knowing: **use boxed types in
validated-input DTOs whenever `@NotNull` needs to actually mean something.**
The `-90..90` / `-180..180` bounds are a real geographic sanity check, not
an arbitrary range.

### 3.4 `CollegeService.java`

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class CollegeService {
    private final CollegeRepository collegeRepository;

    public List<CollegeResponse> findAll() {
        return collegeRepository.findAll().stream().map(CollegeResponse::from).toList();
    }

    public CollegeResponse create(CollegeRequest request) {
        College college = new College();
        apply(college, request);
        return CollegeResponse.from(collegeRepository.save(college));
    }

    public CollegeResponse update(Long id, CollegeRequest request) {
        College college = getEntity(id);
        apply(college, request);
        return CollegeResponse.from(collegeRepository.save(college));
    }

    public void delete(Long id) {
        if (!collegeRepository.existsById(id)) {
            throw new ResourceNotFoundException("College not found: " + id);
        }
        collegeRepository.deleteById(id);
    }

    College getEntity(Long id) {
        return collegeRepository.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("College not found: " + id));
    }

    private void apply(College college, CollegeRequest request) {
        college.setName(request.name());
        college.setCity(request.city());
        college.setLatitude(request.latitude());
        college.setLongitude(request.longitude());
    }
}
```
`create` and `update` share a private `apply(...)` helper to avoid
duplicating the five field-copy lines — this pattern repeats in
`HostelService.applyBasicFields`. `getEntity` is package-private (no
modifier), intentionally reused by `HostelService` when it needs to resolve
a `collegeId` filter parameter into a real `College` (see 4.6) — a small,
deliberate cross-package coupling rather than duplicating the "find or 404"
logic.

### 3.5 `CollegeController.java`

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/colleges")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class CollegeController {
    private final CollegeService collegeService;

    @GetMapping
    public List<CollegeResponse> findAll() {
        return collegeService.findAll();
    }

    @PostMapping
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public CollegeResponse create(@Valid @RequestBody CollegeRequest request) {
        return collegeService.create(request);
    }

    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public CollegeResponse update(@PathVariable Long id, @Valid @RequestBody CollegeRequest request) {
        return collegeService.update(id, request);
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)
    public void delete(@PathVariable Long id) {
        collegeService.delete(id);
    }
}
```
`GET` has no `@PreAuthorize` — it's public (backed up at the URL level by
`SecurityConfig`'s `permitAll()` for `GET /api/colleges/**`). The three
mutating endpoints are each individually guarded with
`@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")` — this is **defense in depth**: even
if `SecurityConfig`'s URL matcher were ever misconfigured to accidentally
allow `POST` through, the method-level check on the controller itself is a
second, independent gate.

