## 4. `hostel` package — search, filtering, distance, images

### 4.1 `Hostel.java`

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "hostel")
@Getter @Setter @NoArgsConstructor
public class Hostel {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false) private String name;
    @Column(columnDefinition = "text") private String description;
    @Column(nullable = false, length = 500) private String address;
    @Column(nullable = false) private String city;
    @Column(nullable = false) private double latitude;
    @Column(nullable = false) private double longitude;

    @Column(name = "price_per_month", nullable = false) private BigDecimal pricePerMonth;
    @Column(name = "food_available", nullable = false) private boolean foodAvailable;
    @Column(name = "total_capacity", nullable = false) private int totalCapacity;
    @Column(name = "available_capacity", nullable = false) private int availableCapacity;
    @Column(name = "contact_phone") private String contactPhone;

    @Column(name = "created_at", nullable = false, updatable = false) private Instant createdAt = Instant.now();
    @Column(name = "updated_at", nullable = false) private Instant updatedAt = Instant.now();

    @PreUpdate
    public void onUpdate() { this.updatedAt = Instant.now(); }
}
```
`pricePerMonth` is `BigDecimal`, not `double` — this is deliberate:
floating-point binary representation can't exactly represent most decimal
fractions (e.g. `0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3` in IEEE 754), which is unacceptable for
money. `BigDecimal` maps to Postgres's exact `numeric(10,2)` type. Distance
and lat/long *are* `double`, because approximate geographic math is fine —
nobody needs their distance-from-college accurate to 15 decimal places.

`@PreUpdate` is a JPA lifecycle callback: Hibernate calls this method
automatically immediately before issuing an `UPDATE` for this entity,
keeping `updatedAt` accurate without every service method having to
remember to set it manually.

### 4.2 `HostelRepository.java` — the atomic capacity operations

```java
public interface HostelRepository extends JpaRepository<Hostel, Long>, JpaSpecificationExecutor<Hostel> {

    @Modifying
    @Query("update Hostel h set h.availableCapacity = h.availableCapacity - 1 " +
            "where h.id = :id and h.availableCapacity > 0")
    int tryDecrementAvailableCapacity(Long id);

    @Modifying
    @Query("update Hostel h set h.availableCapacity = h.availableCapacity + 1 " +
            "where h.id = :id and h.availableCapacity < h.totalCapacity")
    int incrementAvailableCapacity(Long id);
}
```
`JpaSpecificationExecutor<Hostel>` adds `findAll(Specification<Hostel>)` and
the paginated overload — this is what lets `HostelSpecifications` (§4.3)
compose dynamic `WHERE` clauses without hand-written JPQL string
concatenation.

The two `@Query` methods are hand-written JPQL (`update Hostel h set ...` —
note it references the *entity* name `Hostel` and its *field* names like
`h.availableCapacity`, not the table/column names — JPQL operates at the
object-model level, Hibernate translates it to real SQL against
`hostel`/`available_capacity`). `@Modifying` is required on any `@Query`
that isn't a `SELECT` — without it, Spring Data JPA would try to interpret
the query as returning entities and fail.

**Why these two methods exist at all, instead of a normal
`findById` + `setAvailableCapacity` + `save`**: this is the fix for the
booking-confirmation race condition — see §5 (BookingService) for the full
explanation, since that's where these methods get called and where the
concurrency bug they prevent is easiest to demonstrate concretely.

### 4.3 `HostelSpecifications.java` — composable dynamic filters

```java
public final class HostelSpecifications {
    private HostelSpecifications() {}

    public static Specification<Hostel> minPrice(BigDecimal minPrice) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> minPrice == null ? null
                : cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("pricePerMonth"), minPrice);
    }

    public static Specification<Hostel> maxPrice(BigDecimal maxPrice) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> maxPrice == null ? null
                : cb.lessThanOrEqualTo(root.get("pricePerMonth"), maxPrice);
    }

    public static Specification<Hostel> foodAvailable(Boolean food) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> food == null ? null
                : cb.equal(root.get("foodAvailable"), food);
    }

    public static Specification<Hostel> city(String city) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> (city == null || city.isBlank()) ? null
                : cb.equal(cb.lower(root.get("city")), city.toLowerCase());
    }

    public static Specification<Hostel> withinBoundingBox(double centerLat, double centerLon,
                                                            double latDelta, double lonDelta) {
        return (root, query, cb) -> cb.and(
                cb.between(root.get("latitude"), centerLat - latDelta, centerLat + latDelta),
                cb.between(root.get("longitude"), centerLon - lonDelta, centerLon + lonDelta)
        );
    }
}
```
Each method returns a `Specification<Hostel>` — a functional interface
that's really just `(Root, CriteriaQuery, CriteriaBuilder) -> Predicate`,
JPA's Criteria API for building type-safe, composable `WHERE` clauses
without string concatenation (so no SQL-injection surface, and IDE-level
refactoring safety if a field gets renamed).

The `filterValue == null ? null : predicate` pattern is the key idiom:
`Specification.allOf(...)` (used in `HostelService.search`, §4.6) silently
skips any `null` predicate in the list. This is what lets a single
`search(...)` method handle "no filters," "just food," "price range only,"
or "everything at once" without a combinatorial explosion of query methods
or manual `if` branches building a `StringBuilder` of SQL.

`city(...)` lowercases both sides for a case-insensitive match — `"Chennai"`
should match a stored `"chennai"` or `"CHENNAI"`.

`withinBoundingBox` computes a `WHERE lat BETWEEN ... AND long BETWEEN ...`
— a rectangle around a point, cheap to evaluate and indexable on plain
`double` columns (see `idx_hostel_lat_long` in the Flyway migration). This
is currently **defined but not yet wired into `search(...)`** — it exists
as the documented next step for when a `maxDistance` API parameter gets
added (see EXPLANATION.md §1.4 for why it's not urgent yet).

### 4.4 `DistanceService.java`

```java
@Service
public class DistanceService {
    private static final double EARTH_RADIUS_KM = 6371.0;

    public double haversineKm(double lat1, double lon1, double lat2, double lon2) {
        double dLat = Math.toRadians(lat2 - lat1);
        double dLon = Math.toRadians(lon2 - lon1);
        double a = Math.sin(dLat / 2) * Math.sin(dLat / 2)
                + Math.cos(Math.toRadians(lat1)) * Math.cos(Math.toRadians(lat2))
                * Math.sin(dLon / 2) * Math.sin(dLon / 2);
        double c = 2 * Math.atan2(Math.sqrt(a), Math.sqrt(1 - a));
        return EARTH_RADIUS_KM * c;
    }

    public double[] boundingBoxDeltas(double centerLat, double radiusKm) {
        double latDelta = radiusKm / 111.0;
        double lonDelta = radiusKm / (111.0 * Math.max(0.1, Math.cos(Math.toRadians(centerLat))));
        return new double[]{latDelta, lonDelta};
    }
}
```
`haversineKm` is the textbook [haversine
formula](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haversine_formula) for great-circle
distance on a sphere. Worth being able to explain the shape of it if asked:
it converts the lat/long difference into radians, computes the squared
half-chord length `a` between the two points using the haversine trig
identity, then `c = 2·atan2(√a, √(1-a))` is the angular distance in radians
— multiplying by Earth's radius converts that angle into an actual distance.
`atan2` (rather than plain `asin`) is used because it's numerically stable
across the full range of inputs, including near-antipodal points.

`boundingBoxDeltas` converts a search radius in km into lat/long *degree*
deltas: **1 degree of latitude is a constant ~111 km everywhere on Earth**,
but **1 degree of longitude shrinks toward the poles** (the meridians
converge), which is why the longitude delta divides by `cos(latitude)` —
at the equator `cos(0°) = 1` so it's the same as latitude; near the poles
`cos` approaches 0, so the same physical distance spans a much larger
number of longitude degrees. The `Math.max(0.1, ...)` guards against
division blowing up to a huge/infinite delta right at the poles (not a
realistic case for a hostel-finder app, but a defensive floor costs nothing).

