### 4.8 `HostelImage.java`, `HostelImageRepository.java`, `HostelImageService.java`, `HostelImageController.java`

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "hostel_image")
@Getter @Setter @NoArgsConstructor
public class HostelImage {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "hostel_id", nullable = false)
    private Hostel hostel;

    @Column(name = "image_data", nullable = false)
    private byte[] imageData;

    @Column(name = "content_type", nullable = false)
    private String contentType;

    @Column(name = "is_cover", nullable = false)
    private boolean cover;

    @Column(name = "created_at", nullable = false, updatable = false)
    private Instant createdAt = Instant.now();
}
```
**A real bug worth telling as a story**: `imageData` is *not* annotated
`@Lob`. It was originally written with `@Lob @Column(name = "image_data")
private byte[] imageData;` — the intuitive choice, since `@Lob` is JPA's
"large object" annotation. But in this Hibernate version, `@Lob` on a
`byte[]` field maps to Postgres's large-object type (`oid`, a *reference* to
a separate large-object table, PostgreSQL's older BLOB mechanism), while the
Flyway migration created the column as `bytea` (a plain inline binary
column, which is what you actually want for images stored directly in a
row). This mismatch caused Hibernate's schema validation
(`spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto: validate`) to fail app startup entirely
with `SchemaManagementException: wrong column type encountered... found
[bytea], but expecting [oid]`. **The fix was to remove `@Lob`** — Hibernate's
*default* mapping for `byte[]` (no annotation at all) is already `bytea`,
which is what was wanted the whole time. Good interview answer to "tell me
about a bug you hit": explain the *mechanism* (two different Postgres binary
storage strategies, JPA annotation choosing between them), not just "I
removed an annotation and it worked."

```java
public interface HostelImageRepository extends JpaRepository<HostelImage, Long> {
    List<HostelImage> findByHostelId(Long hostelId);
    Optional<HostelImage> findByIdAndHostelId(Long id, Long hostelId);
    Optional<HostelImage> findFirstByHostelIdOrderByCoverDescCreatedAtAsc(Long hostelId);
}
```
`findByIdAndHostelId` — used by the image-serving and delete endpoints to
enforce that an image ID actually belongs to the hostel ID in the URL path
(`/api/hostels/{hostelId}/images/{imageId}`) — without this compound check,
a client could pass any valid `imageId` regardless of which hostel is named
in the path and still get/delete it, since image IDs are globally unique
and don't inherently "belong" to a URL segment without an explicit check.

```java
@Transactional
public HostelImage upload(Long hostelId, MultipartFile file, boolean cover) {
    if (!hostelRepository.existsById(hostelId)) {
        throw new ResourceNotFoundException("Hostel not found: " + hostelId);
    }
    String contentType = file.getContentType();
    if (contentType == null || !ALLOWED_CONTENT_TYPES.contains(contentType)) {
        throw new IllegalRequestException("Unsupported image type: " + contentType);
    }
    Hostel hostelRef = hostelRepository.getReferenceById(hostelId);
    HostelImage image = new HostelImage();
    image.setHostel(hostelRef);
    image.setContentType(contentType);
    image.setCover(cover);
    try {
        image.setImageData(file.getBytes());
    } catch (IOException e) {
        throw new IllegalRequestException("Failed to read uploaded image");
    }
    return hostelImageRepository.save(image);
}
```
`ALLOWED_CONTENT_TYPES` is a `Set.of("image/jpeg", "image/png",
"image/webp")` allow-list — rejecting anything else (e.g. someone trying to
upload an executable renamed to `.png`, or an SVG, which can carry embedded
`<script>` content and is a real XSS vector if ever rendered inline rather
than via `<img>`). Note this checks the **client-supplied** `Content-Type`
header, which a malicious client could lie about — a hardened version would
sniff the actual file bytes (magic-number check) rather than trusting the
header; worth mentioning as a known gap if asked "how would you make this
more secure."

`hostelRepository.getReferenceById(hostelId)` (rather than `findById`) —
this returns a **lazy proxy** without issuing a `SELECT` at all, since we
already confirmed the hostel exists via `existsById` and only need its ID
to set the foreign key on the new `HostelImage` row — fetching the full
`Hostel` entity here would be a wasted query.

```java
@GetMapping("/{imageId}")
public ResponseEntity<byte[]> get(@PathVariable Long hostelId, @PathVariable Long imageId) {
    HostelImage image = hostelImageService.get(hostelId, imageId);
    return ResponseEntity.ok()
            .contentType(MediaType.parseMediaType(image.getContentType()))
            .cacheControl(org.springframework.http.CacheControl.maxAge(7, TimeUnit.DAYS))
            .body(image.getImageData());
}
```
Sets `Cache-Control: max-age=604800` (7 days) — since a hostel image is
essentially immutable content once uploaded (there's no "edit image"
endpoint, only upload/delete), telling the browser to cache it aggressively
avoids re-fetching the same BLOB from Postgres on every page view — this is
the concrete mitigation mentioned in EXPLANATION.md §1.7 for the "no CDN"
tradeoff of storing images in the database.

