## 5. `booking` package — the concurrency-critical part

### 5.1 `BookingStatus.java`

```java
public enum BookingStatus {
    PENDING,
    CONFIRMED,
    REJECTED,
    CANCELLED
}
```
Four states, and the *only* legal transitions (enforced in `BookingService`,
not by the enum itself) are: `PENDING → CONFIRMED`, `PENDING → REJECTED`,
and `PENDING|CONFIRMED → CANCELLED`. There's deliberately no
`REJECTED → CONFIRMED` or any other path — once a booking leaves `PENDING`
via reject, it's terminal.

### 5.2 `Booking.java`

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "booking")
@Getter @Setter @NoArgsConstructor
public class Booking {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id", nullable = false)
    private AppUser user;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "hostel_id", nullable = false)
    private Hostel hostel;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    @Column(nullable = false, length = 20)
    private BookingStatus status = BookingStatus.PENDING;

    @Column(name = "move_in_date", nullable = false) private LocalDate moveInDate;
    @Column(length = 1000) private String notes;
    @Column(name = "created_at", nullable = false, updatable = false) private Instant createdAt = Instant.now();
    @Column(name = "updated_at", nullable = false) private Instant updatedAt = Instant.now();

    @PreUpdate
    public void onUpdate() { this.updatedAt = Instant.now(); }
}
```
`@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)` — stores `"PENDING"` etc. as text in the
database, not the enum's ordinal integer (`EnumType.ORDINAL`, the other
option). This is a deliberate and important choice: with `ORDINAL`, if
someone ever reorders the `BookingStatus` enum constants (e.g. inserts a new
status in the middle), every existing row in the database would silently
mean something different, because the stored integer's meaning depends on
declaration order in the Java source. `STRING` is immune to that — it's the
right default for any enum backing persisted data. Both association fields
(`user`, `hostel`) are `FetchType.LAZY` — this is what causes the
`LazyInitializationException` bug discussed in §5.4.

