        return new BookingResponse(
                b.getId(), b.getHostel().getId(), b.getHostel().getName(), b.getUser().getId(),
                b.getStatus(), b.getMoveInDate(), b.getNotes(), b.getCreatedAt(), b.getUpdatedAt()
        );
    }
}
```
`@FutureOrPresent` on `moveInDate` — rejects a booking request for a date
in the past at the validation layer, before any service logic runs.
`BookingResponse.from(b)` is the method whose `b.getHostel().getName()` call
is exactly where the lazy-loading bug (§5.4) manifested — worth pointing at
this specific line when telling that story.

### 5.4 `BookingService.java` — the full concurrency and lifecycle story

```java
@Transactional
public BookingResponse create(Long userId, BookingRequest request) {
    Hostel hostel = hostelRepository.findById(request.hostelId())
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Hostel not found: " + request.hostelId()));

    Booking booking = new Booking();
    booking.setUser(appUserRepository.getReferenceById(userId));
    booking.setHostel(hostel);
    booking.setMoveInDate(request.moveInDate());
    booking.setNotes(request.notes());
    booking.setStatus(BookingStatus.PENDING);
    return BookingResponse.from(bookingRepository.save(booking));
}
```
Notice **capacity is not touched here at all** — creating a booking never
checks or decrements `availableCapacity`. This is the "booking request vs
reservation" distinction from EXPLANATION.md §1.5: a `PENDING` booking is
just a record of interest, not a hold on a bed. `userId` comes from
`appUserRepository.getReferenceById(userId)` (a lazy proxy, no query — we
trust the ID from the authenticated JWT principal, no need to fetch the full
student record just to set a foreign key), while `hostel` is the *fully
fetched* entity (`findById`, not `getReferenceById`) because we need to
confirm it actually exists (404 if not) before creating a booking against it.

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<BookingResponse> listForUser(Long userId) {
    return bookingRepository.findByUserIdOrderByCreatedAtDesc(userId).stream()
            .map(BookingResponse::from)
            .toList();
}
```
**This is the method that had the real bug.** Originally this method had no
`@Transactional` annotation at all. Here's the exact mechanism, worth being
able to explain precisely:

- The project sets `spring.jpa.open-in-view: false` in `application.yml` —
  a deliberate choice (the Spring Boot default is actually `true`, and
  disabling it is considered best practice, because leaving it `true` keeps
  a database connection/session open for the *entire* HTTP request
  lifecycle just in case a view or serializer touches a lazy field, which
  masks N+1 query bugs and wastes connection-pool capacity).
- With `open-in-view: false`, a Hibernate session only exists for the
  duration of an active `@Transactional` method. `bookingRepository
  .findByUserIdOrderByCreatedAtDesc(userId)` is itself a Spring Data
  repository call, which — absent an enclosing transaction — runs in its
  *own* short-lived, auto-created transaction that commits (and closes its
  session) the moment the query returns.
- Without `@Transactional` on `listForUser` itself, the `.stream().map
  (BookingResponse::from)` line runs **after** that session has already
  closed. `BookingResponse.from` calls `b.getHostel().getName()` —
  `getHostel()` returns a Hibernate lazy proxy (since `Booking.hostel` is
  `FetchType.LAZY`), and calling `.getName()` on an uninitialized proxy
  with no active session throws `org.hibernate.LazyInitializationException:
  could not initialize proxy - no session`.
- **The fix**: add `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` to `listForUser` (and
  identically to `listAll`, §5.5) — this makes the repository query *and*
  the subsequent DTO-mapping stream run inside the *same* transaction/
  session, so the lazy proxy can still fetch on demand when touched.
  `readOnly = true` is an optimization hint (Hibernate can skip dirty-checking
  since nothing will be modified) appropriate for a pure read method.
- **This was found by actually running the app**, not by a unit test — the
  Playwright-driven browser walkthrough hit `GET /api/bookings/me` for real,
  and the Spring Boot console logged the exact stack trace, which is what
  made the root cause traceable. Good evidence, if asked, of debugging by
  reading real logs from a real running system rather than guessing.

```java
@Transactional
public BookingResponse cancel(Long userId, Long bookingId) {
    Booking booking = getOwnedBooking(userId, bookingId);
    if (booking.getStatus() != BookingStatus.PENDING && booking.getStatus() != BookingStatus.CONFIRMED) {
        throw new IllegalRequestException("Only pending or confirmed bookings can be cancelled");
    }
    if (booking.getStatus() == BookingStatus.CONFIRMED) {
        hostelRepository.incrementAvailableCapacity(booking.getHostel().getId());
    }
    booking.setStatus(BookingStatus.CANCELLED);
    return BookingResponse.from(bookingRepository.save(booking));
}
```
The capacity-restoring branch only fires if the booking being cancelled was
`CONFIRMED` — a `PENDING` cancellation never touched capacity in the first
place (per `create()` above), so there's nothing to give back. This is the
mirror image of `confirm()` below, using `incrementAvailableCapacity`
(§4.2) — also atomic, also guarded (`available_capacity < total_capacity`)
so a bug elsewhere can never push capacity above the hostel's real total.

```java
@Transactional
public BookingResponse confirm(Long bookingId) {
    Booking booking = getEntity(bookingId);
    if (booking.getStatus() != BookingStatus.PENDING) {
        throw new IllegalRequestException("Only pending bookings can be confirmed");
    }
    int updated = hostelRepository.tryDecrementAvailableCapacity(booking.getHostel().getId());
    if (updated == 0) {
        throw new IllegalRequestException("Hostel is at full capacity");
    }
    booking.setStatus(BookingStatus.CONFIRMED);
    return BookingResponse.from(bookingRepository.save(booking));
}
```
**This is the single most interview-worthy method in the codebase.** Walk
through it as: "here's a check-then-act race condition, and here's how I
closed it."

The naive, *wrong* version most people write first:
```java
// WRONG — race condition:
Hostel h = hostelRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow(...);
if (h.getAvailableCapacity() > 0) {          // (1) CHECK
    h.setAvailableCapacity(h.getAvailableCapacity() - 1);  // (2) ACT
    hostelRepository.save(h);
}
```
If two `confirm()` calls for the same 1-bed-remaining hostel run
concurrently, both can execute step (1) and both see `availableCapacity ==
1` *before either one has written back the decremented value* — this is
the textbook **TOCTOU (time-of-check to time-of-use)** race. Both then
proceed to step (2), and whichever `save()` commits last simply overwrites
the other's write — the hostel ends up "confirmed" for two different
bookings while capacity only ever accounted for one bed. This kind of bug
is notoriously hard to catch in manual testing because it only manifests
under real concurrent load, not sequential clicking.

The actual, correct implementation instead calls
`hostelRepository.tryDecrementAvailableCapacity(...)` (§4.2), whose body is:
```sql
update hostel set available_capacity = available_capacity - 1
where id = :id and available_capacity > 0
```
This collapses "check" and "act" into **one atomic SQL statement**. The
database's row-level locking guarantees that if two transactions try to run
this `UPDATE` against the same row concurrently, they get serialized — the
second one's `WHERE available_capacity > 0` is evaluated against
whatever value the *first* transaction already committed, not a stale value
read earlier. If the first transaction already brought it to `0`, the
second transaction's `WHERE` clause matches zero rows, and the method
returns `0` (the JPA `@Modifying` query's return value is the affected row
count) instead of `1`. `BookingService.confirm` reads that return value
directly — `if (updated == 0) throw ...` — so **the database itself is the
concurrency control**, not a lock or `synchronized` block in the Java code.
This is deliberately simpler and more robust than application-level
locking: it works correctly even across multiple application server
instances (a `synchronized` block only protects against races *within one
JVM*), which matters the moment this app is ever horizontally scaled.

This was verified empirically, not just reasoned about: a manual test
created two bookings against a hostel with `totalCapacity = 1`, confirmed
the first (succeeded, capacity → 0), then attempted to confirm the second
— which deterministically returned `409 Hostel is at full capacity` every
time, not "occasionally," which is exactly the signature of a correctly
closed race condition versus one that merely usually works.

```java
private Booking getOwnedBooking(Long userId, Long bookingId) {
    Booking booking = getEntity(bookingId);
    if (!booking.getUser().getId().equals(userId)) {
        throw new ResourceNotFoundException("Booking not found: " + bookingId);
    }
    return booking;
}
```
Notice this throws `ResourceNotFoundException` (404), not an
`AccessDeniedException` (403), when a student tries to cancel someone
else's booking. This is a deliberate choice sometimes called **not leaking
existence** — a 403 confirms to the caller "this resource exists, you're
just not allowed to touch it," which is itself information disclosure for
a resource scoped to a specific user. Returning 404 makes "it's not yours"
indistinguishable from "it doesn't exist at all" from the outside.

### 5.5 `BookingController.java`

```java
@PostMapping
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('STUDENT')")
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
public BookingResponse create(@AuthenticationPrincipal UserPrincipal principal,
                               @Valid @RequestBody BookingRequest request) {
    return bookingService.create(principal.getId(), request);
}
```
`@AuthenticationPrincipal UserPrincipal principal` — Spring Security
resolves this parameter automatically from whatever `JwtAuthFilter` (§2.1)
placed into `SecurityContextHolder` for this request. This is how a
controller method gets "who is calling me" without ever touching the
`Authorization` header or JWT parsing directly — that's all abstracted away
by the filter chain running before the controller is even invoked.

Every mutating endpoint pairs a `@PreAuthorize` role check with the right
data-scoping: `create`/`listMine`/`cancel` require `STUDENT` and always
operate on `principal.getId()` (never a client-supplied user ID — a student
can never act as another student, by construction, not by a runtime check);
`listAll`/`confirm`/`reject` require `ADMIN` and aren't scoped to a
particular user at all, since admins can see/act on everything.

